<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#hippeis">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hippeis</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as hippeus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hippeis (singular hippeus) is the Greek term for cavalry, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> after the reforms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solon">Solon</a> the name of the second highest of the four social classes: men with a yearly income of at least 300 medimnoi, enough to buy and keep a war horse. The Athenian cavalry, formed after the Greco-Persian War in the 5th century BCE, grew from 300 men to 1,200 and was commanded by two hipparchs, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boule-ancient-greece">boule</a> examined its members and their equipment. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> the hippeis were the royal guard of honour, 300 youths under thirty who served as heavy infantry in the king's bodyguard. Their counterparts were the Roman equites and medieval knights.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>hippeis</b>, or knights, were the second class: men whose land yielded at least 300 medimnoi a year, enough, in Aristotle’s account, to keep a horse.</blockquote><a href="/societies/athens">Social Hierarchy of Classical Athens</a></li></ul></section>
